              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#lecture_notes_and_expositions"} Lecture notes and expositions
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#original_articles_on_the_general_notion"} Original articles on the general notion
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#original_articles_on_special_cases"} Original articles on special cases
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            the basic entities that are being described are called
            = succeed "," do
              %em fields
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %h3#general General
          %p
            The basic example that probably gives the whole concept its name is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+field"} electric field
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+chart"} coordinate chart
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            splits into the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+field"} electric field
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
            which are both modeled by a
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+field"} vector field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec E
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec E(x)
            of the vector field at a given point of spacetime is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector"} vector
            that expresses the magnitude and direction of the electric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            that is exerted on an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+charge"} electrically charged
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            In fact more fundamentally, if we do not specify a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+chart"} coordinate chart
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            is not in fact represented by two vector fields. Rather, its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            is represented by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-form"} differential 2-form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+field"} tensor field
            of rank
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            of degree-2 in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle bundle with connection
          %p
            Or for instance the field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            if modeled as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+field"} tensor field
            of rank
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,0)
            – but subject to the constraint that this be pointwise non-degenerate. More fundamentally the field of gravity is instead a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vielbein+field"} vielbein field
          %p
            Similar statements hold for all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forces"} forces
            of nature, such as the force of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+nuclear+force"} weak nuclear force
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"} strong nuclear force
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connections
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strengths"} field strengths
            are rank
            = succeed "-tensor" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,2)
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            and the field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            are the physical fields that historically gave rise to what is now called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            in physics is constituted by fields in a similar sense. Specifically, where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            fields in physics are usually
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connections on a bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundles"} associated bundles
          %p
            Field theory was originally discovered as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            of fields on
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
            consisting of a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            propagating in a
            %em fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is described by a field theory. In this case the field is not defined on spacetime, but on the abstract
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            of the particle, say the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectory"} trajectory
            of the particle, is then a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \;\colon\; \mathbb{R}\to X
            %em field
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            and is then called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            field. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            of a single particle may be equivalently thought of as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            on the 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            of the particle.
          %p
            This perspective generalizes. Next one can consider fields on 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surfaces"} surfaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_2
            which again are given by maps into some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is then said to describe not a particle but a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            propagating in spacetime, defined on the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            of the particle. For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            of dimension 3 one accordingly speaks of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membrane"} membrane
            and then for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            of general dimension here one speaks of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} brane
          %p
            But there is no fundamental distinction between physical fields on spaces that are interpreted as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            and those that are interpreted as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolumes"} worldvolumes
            of objects propagating
            %em in
            a fixed spacetime. In general these notions mix. For instance the full description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+particles"} relativistic particles
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+strings"} relativistic strings
            involves a field that is really a field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            that arise by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
            of higher dimensional theories typically have “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"}> scalar moduli fields
            ” that used to be components of the field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            in higher dimensions but now after compactifications become maps into some auxiliary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            fields.
          %h3#AFirstIdeaOfQuantumFields A first idea of quantum fields
          %p
            We introduce here the basic concepts of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
            and then for its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            In full beauty these concepts are extremely general; but in this section the aim is to give a first good idea of the subject, and therefore we present for the moment only a restricted setup, notably assuming that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            (see below) is an ordinary and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+bundle"} trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            and that all fields are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boson"} bosonic
          %p
            This does subsume what is considered in most traditional texts on the subject. In subsequent sections we will eventually discuss more general situations, notably we will eventually allow spacetime to be any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+Lorentzian+manifold"} globally hyperbolic Lorentzian manifold
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            to be an super
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Lie+algebroid"} infinity-Lie algebroid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV+formalism"} BRST-BV quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            coupled to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetime"} on curved spacetimes
          %p
            Throughout we use the case of the real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
            as an illustrative running example, which we develop alongside with the theory. The discussion of other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            species that are of more genuine interest in applications is postponed to their dedicated sections below.
          %h4#spacetime Spacetime
          %p Throughout, let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  p \in \mathbb{N}
          %p
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            and write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Sigma \coloneqq \mathbb{R}^{p,1} \coloneqq (\mathbb{R}^{p+1}, \eta)
          %p
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p+1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{p+1}
            of dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p+1
            equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+invariant+differential+form"} constant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            which at the origin is given by the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+form"} quadratic form
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature+of+a+quadratic+form"} signature
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (-, +, \cdots, +)
          %p
            in terms of the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+functions"} coordinate functions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi k
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x^k \;\colon\; \mathbb{R}^{p+1} \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
          %p
            which we index starting at zero:
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi k
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x^k)_{k = 0}^p
          %p We write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi dvol
                    %mi Σ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∶−
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  dvol_\Sigma
                  \\;\coloneq\;
                  d x^0 \wedge d x^1 \wedge \cdots \wedge d x^p
                  \\in \Omega^{p+1}(\mathbb{R}^{p,1})
          %p
            for the induced
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d x^0 \in \Omega^1(\Sigma)
          %p
            the canonical representative of the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time+orientation"} time orientation
            on Minkowski spacetime.
          %h4#fields Fields
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
              configuration
            on a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is meant to be some kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantity"} quantity
            assigned to each point of spacetime (each
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/event"} event
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            configuration is at each point of spacetime a collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vectors"} vectors
            that encode the direction in which a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charged+particle"} charged particle
            passing through that point will feel a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            (the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+force"} Lorentz force
          %p This is readily formalized: If
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi SmthMfd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F \in SmthMfd
          %p
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            of “values” that the given kind of field may take at any spacetime point, then a field configuration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            is modeled as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            from spacetime to this space of values:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi F
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Phi
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\Sigma
                  \\longrightarrow
                  F
                  \\,.
          %p
            It will be useful to unify spacetime and the space of field values into a single space, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E \;\coloneqq\; \Sigma \times F
          %p
            and to think of this equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            map onto the first factor as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            of spaces of field values over spacetime
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≔
                        %mtd
                          %mi Σ
                          %mo ×
                          %mi F
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi fb
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↙
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi pr
                                  %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Σ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  E &amp;\coloneqq&amp; \Sigma \times F
                  \\\
                  {}^{\mathllap{fb}}\downarrow &amp; \swarrow_{\mathrlap{pr_1}}
                  \\\
                  \\Sigma
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is then called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            of field values is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            of this
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+fiber"} field fiber
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              %strong (fields)
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi E
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mi fb
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{E \\ \downarrow^{\mathrlap{fb}} \\ \Sigma }
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
                configuration
              (of type specified by this field bundle) is a
              %em
                smooth
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
              of this
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
              of the form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi \colon \Sigma \longrightarrow E
              such that composed with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
              map it is the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+function"} identity function
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi fb
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi Φ
                      %mo =
                      %mi id
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} fb \circ \Phi = id
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi E
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi Φ
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi fb
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Σ
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mi Σ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; E
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{\Phi}}\nearrow &amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{fb}}
                    \\\
                    \\Sigma &amp; = &amp; \Sigma
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              The
              %em
                field
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+set"} smooth
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+sections"} space of all these
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi SmoothSet
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Gamma_\Sigma(E) \in SmoothSet
                    \\,.
            %p
              This is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              of all field configurations
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
              as above, and it is equipped with the structure of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+set"} smooth set
              by declaring that a
              %em smooth family
              of field configurations, parameterized over any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is a smooth function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                            %mo ×
                            %mi Σ
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi Φ
                                  %mrow
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi E
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi u
                            %mo ,
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi Φ
                              %mi u
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    U \times \Sigma &amp;\overset{\Phi_{(-)}(-)}{\longrightarrow}&amp; E
                    \\\
                    (u,x) &amp;\mapsto&amp; \Phi_u(x)
                    }
            %p
              such that for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \in U
              we have
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi Φ
                        %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi id
                        %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \circ \Phi_{u}(-) = id_\Sigma
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi E
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi Φ
                                    %mrow
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi fb
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                            %mo ×
                            %mi Σ
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi pr
                                  %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mi Σ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; E
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{\Phi_{(-)}(-)}}\nearrow &amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{fb}}
                    \\\
                    U \times \Sigma &amp;\underset{pr_2}{\longrightarrow}&amp; \Sigma
                    }
                    \\,.
          #TrivialVectorBundleAsAFieldBundle.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+vector+bundle"} trivial vector bundle
                as a
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            %p
              In applications the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+fiber"} field fiber
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is often a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimensional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
              and equipped with the structure of a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is of course a
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+vector+bundle"} trivial
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            %p
              Choosing any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} linear basis
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi a
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi^a)_{a = 1}^s
              of the field fiber, then over
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
              we have canonical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinates"} coordinates
              on the total space of the field bundle
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi μ
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi a
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    ( (x^\mu)_{\mu = 0}^p, ( \phi^a )_{a = 1}^s )
                    \\,.
          #RealScalarFieldBundle.num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+scalar+field"}> real scalar field
                )
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              and if
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F \coloneqq \mathbb{R}
            %p
              is simply the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            .maruku-equation
