            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to X
            over the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (or better: naturally over all spacetimes, see at
            %em
              %a{:href => "#IdeaLocality"} Locality
            below) called the
            %strong
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            and that a field configuration of the system is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of this field bundle. This is for instance the basis for the theory of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+bicomplex"} variational bicomplex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
            for expressing
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+spaces"} covariant phase spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+geometry"} multisymplectic geometry
          %p While this goes in the right direction, it cannot be quite the final answer, as it misses crucial properties that are demanded of a general notion of field. We now discuss these problems:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#IdeaLargeGaugeTransformations"} Large gauge transformations
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#IdeaLocality"} Locality
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#IdeaSpinStructures"} Spin structures and other G-structures
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#BackgroundFields"} Background fields
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#HigherGaugeFields"} Higher gauge fields
          %p
            In the course of discussing the problems we also motivate and indicate their solution by a more natural notion of field moduli in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            = succeed "-section" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#Definition"} Definition
          %h4#IdeaLargeGaugeTransformations Large gauge transformations
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            specifically but in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            generally, physical fields come equipped with a notion of which
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+histories"} field histories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalent
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+equivalence"} gauge equivalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
            of gauge equivalent fields. This means that, generically, for any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+theory"} physical theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            of some
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            and generally an
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            of field histories of which the sections of the field bundle only form the space of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
            form the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+transformations"} higher gauge transformations
            of order
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            form the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-morphisms"} n-morphisms
          %p
            To some extent this is dealt with in traditional
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variational calculus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            on the space of field configurations,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
            spits out a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+L-%E2%88%9E+algebroid"} derived L-∞ algebroid
            whose objects are field configurations, and whose 1-cells are infinitesimal invariances of the given
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
          %p
            This goes in the right direction – it is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+differentiation"} Lie differentiation
            of the more encompassing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} smooth ∞-groupoid
            of fields and gauge transformations – but has several problems, the main one being that it does not know about
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+gauge+transformations"} large gauge transformations
          %p
            Famous examples of the importance of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+gauge+transformations"} large gauge transformations
            appear in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"}> 2d CFT
              , for which all standard theory would break down if the global
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+transformations"} conformal transformations
              were not considered as gauge transformations.
          %h4#IdeaLocality Locality
          %p
            Fields defined as sections of field bundles cannot capture gauge phenomena in a
            %em local
            way, as is necessary for a manifestly local formulation such in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+prequantum+field+theory"} extended prequantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+quantum+field+theory"} extended quantum field theory
            (sometimes called the “multi-tiered” formulation).
          %p
            Specifically, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
              configuration
            – is a combination of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+sector"} instanton sector
            – modeled by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} equivalence class
            of a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            – and the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potential"}> gauge potential
            ”, modeled by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on this bundle
            (see below at
            %em
              %a{:href => "#GaugeFields"} Gauge fields
            for details). There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(P) \to X
            such that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            are precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connections
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi c
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_{c} E(P_c) \to X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            ranges over the instanton sectors, is a field bundle for Yang-Mills fields on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            But this construction is not local: if we consider this assignment of field bundles to all suitable manifolds
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to X
            is a cover of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            by gluing the field bundle on the cover. This is because
            %em locally
            every
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            has trivial class, so that locally there is always only a single (the trivial) instanton sector.
          %p
            This failure of locality is often not recognized in the literature, since many if not most descriptions of physics restrict to trivial spacetime topology and/or restrict to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            only. A formulation accurate and encompassing enough to see this issue is
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} AQFT on curved spacetimes
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "field+bundle#BDS"} Benini-Dappiaggi-Schenkel 13
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "field+bundle#Schenkel14"} Schenkel 14
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            equipped with a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            that assigns the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
            of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+fields"} Yang-Mills fields
            built from the field bundle of connections on the given principal bundles; and they observe that the result fails to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net"} local net
            in that the inclusion of observables of a smaller spacetime into a larger patch may fail the isotony axiom (
            %a{:href => "field+bundle#BDS"}> BDS, remark 5.6
            ). The authors then try to circumvent this by restricting to trivial instanton sectors. The fix later appears in (
            %a{:href => "#BeniniSchenkelSzabo15"}> Benini-Schenkel-Szabo 15
            ), where the authors then consider proper
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
            of fields.
          %p
            But notice that instanton sectors is a non-negligible phenomenon. For instance the very
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superposition"} superposition
            of all possible instanton sectors (see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+in+QCD"} instanton in QCD
            for more on this). And there are field theories where the fields consist entirely of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+sectors"}> instanton sectors
            ” and where there is no infinitesimal information about the gauge group at all: these are theories whose gauge group is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
            and its higher analogy such as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yetter+model"} Yetter model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
            a local field bundle formalism can see nothing of the actual fields and also traditional tools applied to a global field bundle (such as traditional
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Definition"} below
          %p
            But this example already points to the general nature of the problem with field bundles, and also to its solution: while the
            = succeed "-component" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton"} instanton
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+fields"} Yang-Mills fields
            are not section of a bundle, they famously are sections of a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            – the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            of
            = succeed "-principal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
          %p
            The problem with the locality of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            is solved by passing from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles"} fiber bundles
            to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} fiber ∞-bundles
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} higher differential geometry
            there is an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G_{conn}
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connections"} principal connections
            (being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stackification"} stackification
            of the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+of+Lie+algebra-valued+forms"} groupoid of Lie algebra-valued forms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \mathbf{B}G_{conn}
            are equivalent to Yang-Mills fields on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (even including their
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} fiber ∞-bundles
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is even a trivial field bundle, namely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi conn
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \times \mathbf{B}G_{conn} \to X
          %p
            out of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of fields.
          %p
            This is a differential refinement of what is called the trivial
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbe"} gerbe
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \times \mathbf{B}G \to X
          %p and hence the “field bundle for instanton sectors” of Yang-Mills fields.
          %p
            In summary: there cannot be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            such that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+sections"} local sections
            is the sheaf of configurations of the Yang-Mills field. But there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+infinity-bundle"} fiber 2-bundle
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            of sections is the stack of configurations of the Yang-Mills field.
          %p
            Judging from these examples one might be tempted to guess that the notion of field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            should simply be replaced by that of field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} fiber ∞-bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Fields
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Fields}
            of fields, which for
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            is simply
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Fields
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi conn
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{Fields} = \mathbf{B}G_{conn}
                  \\,.
          %p
            This perspective, which we describe in detail
            %a{:href => "#Definition"} below
            also has the pleasant effect that it drastically simplifies and unifies notions of quantum field theory, for this says equivalently that if only we allow spaces in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            is a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            quantum field theory: one whose fields are simply maps to a given
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
          %p But there are more advantages, slightly less obvious. These we come to in the following points.
          %h4#IdeaSpinStructures
            Spin-structures and other
            = succeed "-structures" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Some fields in physics are (or involve) choices of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-structure"} G-structure
            in the sense of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduction+and+lift+of+structure+groups"} reduction and lift of structure groups
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientation
            and of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spin+structure"} Spin structure
            in field theories with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            fields (discussed in detail in
            %em
              %a{:href => "#Fermions"} Fermions
            below). Often in the literature the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientation
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spin+structure"} Spin structure
            is treated as an external parameter, but detailed analysis at least in low-dimensional examples shows that in the full theory this is really a field configuration. For instance in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            for theories with fermions, part of the integral over all field configurations is a sum over Spin structures.
          %p
            Now, a spin structure
            %em is
            equivalently a section of something, but again not of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundle"} principal 2-bundle
          %p
            To see how this works, first recall the case of orientations, whose description as sections of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+bundle"} orientation bundle
            is familiar.
          %p
            For a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            represented by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi GL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tau_X \;\colon\; X \to \mathbf{B}GL(n)
          %p
            be the map that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulating+morphism"} modulates
            its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            (discussed at
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics#TangentBundle"}
                %em geometry of physics - tangent bundle
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+spaces"} classifying spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks"} moduli stacks
            for various other groups (this is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+tower"} Whitehead tower
            of
            = succeed "):" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}O(n)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi Spin
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                                %mfrac
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mn 2
                              %msub
                                %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                  %mi p
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mn 3
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi s
                                %mi X
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi SO
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                  %mi w
                                %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mn 2
                          %msub
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi o
                                  %mi X
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi e
                                %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi O
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                  %mi w
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %msub
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi τ
                                  %mi X
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi GL
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \vdots
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}Spin(n) &amp;\stackrel{\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{p}_1}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}^3 U(1)
                  \\\
                  \&amp;\mathllap{s_X}\nearrow&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}SO(n) &amp;\stackrel{\mathbf{w}_2}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}^2 \mathbb{Z}_2
                  \\\
                  \&amp;{}^{\mathllap{o_X}}\nearrow&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  X &amp; \stackrel{e_X}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}O(n) &amp;\stackrel{\mathbf{w}_1}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}\mathbb{Z}_2
                  \\\
                  \&amp;{}_{\mathrlap{\tau_X}} \searrow&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}GL(n)
                  }
          %p
            A lift of the tangent bundle map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau_X
            to a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_X \colon X \to \mathbf{B}O(n)
            as indicated is a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+structure"} orthogonal structure
            (a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vielbein+field"} vielbein field
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#OrdinaryGravity"} Ordinary gravity
          %p
            Then a further lift to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi o
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi SO
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} o_X : X \to \mathbf{B}SO(n)
            is a choice of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi s
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_X : X \to \mathbf{B}Spin(n)
            is a choice of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
          %p
            Now, every hook-shaped sub-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            in the above of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mover
                            %mi G
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi c
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi n
                          %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\mathbf{B}\hat G
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{B}G &amp;\stackrel{\mathbf{c}}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}^n A
                  }
          %p
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+sequence"} homotopy fiber sequence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
            this means that the “space” – really:
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            or just
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \mathbf{B}G
            to a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \mathbf{B}\hat G
            is equivalently the type of trivializations of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composite
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi c
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi n
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \mathbf{B}G \stackrel{\mathbf{c}}{\to} \mathbf{B}^n A
          %p
            Now if we have an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+structure"} orthogonal structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_X : X \to \mathbf{B}O(n)
            given, then this composite map according to the above diagram is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi w
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{w}_1(e_X)
                  \\;\colon\;
                  X \to \mathbf{B}\mathbb{Z}_2
                  \\,.
          %p
            This represents the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Stiefel-Whitney+class"} first Stiefel-Whitney class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi w
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [w_1(\tau_X)] \in H^1(X, \mathbb{Z}_2)
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau_X
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+cover"} double cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat X \to X
            and this is precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+bundle"} orientation bundle
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientation
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Assume then such orientation field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi o
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} o_X
            is given. Then in the next step the relevant composite map is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi w
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi o
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{w}_2(o_X) \;\colon\; X \to \mathbf{B}^2 \mathbb{Z}_2
                  \\,.
          %p
            This now represents the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+Stiefel-Whitney+class"} second Stiefel-Whitney class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi w
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [w_2(\tau_X)] \in H^2(X, \mathbb{Z}_2)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and classifies a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf{B}\mathbb{Z}_2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundle"} principal 2-bundle
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %msub
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\mathbf{B}\mathbb{Z}_2 &amp;\to&amp; P
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; X
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is sometimes called the
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spin
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lifting+bundle+gerbe"} lifting bundle gerbe
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi o
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} o_X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spin+structure"} Spin structure
            is a choice of section of this 2-bundle. Hence spin structures are parts of fields in physics which are not sections of a field 1-bundle. Again, this is faithfully captured only in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
          %p
            This is only the most famous phenomenon in a large class of similar structures of fields in field theory. Notably in higher dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            and in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            there are fields which are ever higher lifts through this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+tower"} Whitehead tower
            –
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+spin+structures"} higher spin structures
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/String+structures"} String structures
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fivebrane+structures"} Fivebrane structures
            in the next two steps. Accordingly, these are fields which are equivalently sections of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+3-bundles"} principal 3-bundles
            (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+circle+3-bundle"}> Chern-Simons circle 3-bundle
            ”) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+infinity-bundle"} principal 7-bundles
            (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+circle+7-bundle"}> Chern-Simons circle 7-bundle
            ”).
          %h4#BackgroundFields Background fields
          %p
            Comparison of the above discussions under
            %em
              %a{:href => "#IdeaLocality"} Locality
            and
            %em
              %a{:href => "#IdeaSpinStructures"} Spin structures
            shows that there we had a higher-geometric field bundle of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+fields"} Yang-Mills fields
            which was hower “trivial” in the sense that it was a projection out of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            with a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            = succeed "-type," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi conn
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon : X \to \mathbf{B}G_{conn}
            = succeed "-type," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
          %p
            But a closer inspection shows that in fact both situations are entirely analogous – once we realize that here these Spin-structure fields are not really defined just on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em equipped with its orientation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi o
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} o_X
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background field
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            incarnated naturally as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos+theory"} higher topos theory
            %em moduli stack of spin structure fields
            and makes spin-structures be
            = succeed "-type" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
          %p
            the natural way to regard both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as well as its orientation structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi o
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} o_X
            as a single object is to regard the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi o
                        %mi X
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi SO
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \stackrel{o_X}{\to} \mathbf{B}SO(n)
            as an object in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} slice (2,1)-topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi SO
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/\mathbf{B}SO(n)}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            is a map of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
            into
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}SO(n)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            filling the evident triangle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi o
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} o_X
            to a spin structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi s
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_X
            as above, hence a diagram of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi s
                                %mi X
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi Spin
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi o
                                  %mi X
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ⇙
                            %mo ≃
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↙
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi SpinStruc
                                  %mi n
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi SO
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  X &amp;&amp;\stackrel{s_X}{\to}&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}Spin(n)
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{o_X}}\searrow &amp;\swArrow_\simeq&amp; \swarrow_{\mathrlap{\mathbf{SpinStruc}_n}}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}SO(n)
                  }
          %p is equivalently a map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi o
                    %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi SpinStruc
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  o_X \to \mathbf{SpinStruc}_n
          %p
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi SO
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/\mathbf{B}SO(n)}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi conn
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \to \mathbf{B}G_{conn}
                  \\,,
          %p
            but in the collection of stacks
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            itself, not in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} slice
          %p
            The slice here encodes the presence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+fields"} background fields
            – namely orientations in this case – whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            in turn is, in this case,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}SO(n)
          %p
            Notice that also the field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            has a background field in this precise sense: as metioned above, a gravitational field configuration is a lift of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau_X
            through
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi OrthStruc
                          %mi n
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi GL
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}O(n) \stackrel{\mathbf{OrthStruc}_n}{\to} \mathbf{B}GL(n)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi OrthStruc
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tau_X \to \mathbf{OrthStruc}_n
          %p
            in the slice
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi GL
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/\mathbf{B}GL(n)}
            %em
              %a{:href => "#OrdinaryGravity"} Ordinary gravity
            below.) Hence also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            becomes a
            = succeed "-type" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau_X
          %p Now, at least for the field of gravity one can of course emulate the fields also by sections of a field bundle (while already for the second next step in the Whitehead tower, Spin structures, this is no longer the case, as we have seen). But even so, the field bundle formalism clearly misses then the relation between fields and background fields.
          %p In particular for two reasons
          %ol
            %li
              %p Typically the presence of background fields indicates that in a more comprehensive discussion background fields are also fields that vary;
            %li
              %p
                Often background fields on one space affect fields on
                %em another
                space.
          %p
            An archetypical example for both these effects combined is 3d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            with a compact, simple and simply connected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            in the presence of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+lines"} Wilson lines
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            on 3-dimensional
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            whose fields are
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
            as for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            above, hence given by maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi conn
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi \colon \Sigma \to \mathbf{B}G_{conn}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            which describes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            propagating around
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knots"} knots
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C : S^1 \to \Sigma
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            for which the restriction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi|_C
            serves as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background gauge field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/\mathbf{B}G_{conn}}
            which in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            is given by a diagram of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo ,
                          %mi 𝔤
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi C
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇙
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↙
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi OrbitStruct
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  S^1 &amp;&amp;\to&amp;&amp; \Omega^1(-,\mathfrak{g}//T)
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{C}}\searrow &amp;\swArrow&amp; \swarrow_{\mathrlap{\mathbf{OrbitStruct}}}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}G_{conn}
                  }
          %p
            for some map on the right which we discuss in detail below in
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#ChernSimonsWithWilsonLines"} Chern-Simons fields with Wilson line fields
          %p
            Here considering just these fields in the background of a fixed
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi|_C
            produced a 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            is that “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+loop"}> Wilson loop
            ” observable of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi|_C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jones+polynomial"} Jones polynomial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+invariant"} knot invariant
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot"} knot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is that one consider also
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            as a dynamical field, not as a fixed background. Indeed, in the full theory of Chern-Simons with Wilson loops that includes both the fields on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            as well as those on the knot, a field configuration is the diagram as above but regarded as the square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
