          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Definition
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FieldsInAnActionFunctional"}
              provides a unified perspective on fields from several perspectives.
            %p
              On the one hand, it almost explicitly says that in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
              all fields are “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
              fields” (see below at
              = succeed "):" do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#SclarModuliFields"} Examples – Scalar and Sigma-model fields
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+%E2%88%9E-stack"} moduli ∞-stack
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Fields
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Fields}
              as the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
              then fields are simply maps from their domain (when regarded as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              %em and
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background field
            %p
              On the other hand, we see below in
              %em
                %a{:href => "#RelationOfFieldsToSections"} Relation to sections of ∞-bundles
              that from another perspective def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FieldsInAnActionFunctional"}
              says that all fields are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
              of an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} associated ∞-bundle
              to an
              = succeed "-bundle" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
              all fields are “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
              fields” (see below at
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#MatterFields"} Matter fields
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge+%28physics%29"} charged
              under the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background gauge field
            %p
              Finally, we see below in
              %em
                %a{:href => "#RelationToTwistedCohomology"} Relation to twisted cohomology
              that from yet another perspective def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FieldsInAnActionFunctional"}
              says that fields are equivalently
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
              in general
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
              %em
                %a{:href => "#ChanPatonGaugeFields"} Examples – Chan-Paton gauge fields
              below), but we see below that it is useful in its full generality. For instance the field of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
              is in a precise sense a 0-cocycle with coefficients in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset+space"} coset space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi GL
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GL(n)/O(n)
              that is twisted by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
              of spacetime (which exhibits the background gauge structure for
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
              of
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+geometry"} type II geometry
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
          %h3#RestrictionAndPullback Restriction and pullback of physical fields
          %p
            It is familiar from basic examples that not every type of physical field on a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            can be pulled back (in the sense of pullback of functions) along any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \;\colon\;Y \to X
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vielbein+field"} vielbein field
            or
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            %em
              %a{:href => "#OrdinaryGravity"} Ordinary graviry
            may be pulled back only along
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+diffeomorphisms"} local diffeomorphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/properties"} properties
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            to pull back a given field and in fact in general one needs
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
          %p
            In view of def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FieldsInAnActionFunctional"}
            above this is immediate: by that definition a field on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in general does not just depend on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Φ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi_X
          #PullbackAlongGeneralizedLocalDiffeomorphisms.num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Since by def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FieldsInAnActionFunctional"}
              a physical field is a map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi Φ
                      %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Fields
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon \Phi_X \to \mathbf{Fields}
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi BgFields
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/\mathbf{BgFields}}
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon Y \to X
              when these are extended to maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Φ
                      %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Φ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi_Y \to \Phi_X
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi BgFields
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/\mathbf{BgFields}}
              of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+fields"} background fields
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagrams"} diagrams
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi Φ
                                    %mi Y
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ⇙
                              %mi κ
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↙
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi Phi
                                    %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi BgFields
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    Y &amp;&amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp;&amp; X
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{\Phi_Y}}\searrow &amp;\swArrow_{\kappa}&amp; \swarrow_{\mathrlap{Phi_X}}
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\mathbf{BgFields}
                    }
                    \\,,
            %p
              hence maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
              equipped with a choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                    %mo ;
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi Φ
                      %mi X
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Φ
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\kappa ;\colon\; f^*\Phi_X \simeq \Phi_Y
            %p
              between the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+fields"} background fields
          %p
            In standard
            %em
              %a{:href => "#Examples"} Examples
            discussed below we see that this is a familar fact. For instance applied to the field of gravity (see
            %em
              %a{:href => "#OrdinaryGravity"} Gravity
            below) it says that the gravitational field can be pulled back precisely along
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+diffeomorphisms"} local diffeomorphisms
            %em
              %a{:href => "#SpinStructures"} Spin structures
            below) can be pulled back along orientation-preserving maps. Or : for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chan-Paton+gauge+fields"} Chan-Paton gauge fields
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#ChanPatonGaugeFields"} Chan-Paton gauge fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            on D-branes known in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %h3#BoundaryAndDefectFields Boundary and defect fields
          %p
            In def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FieldsInAnActionFunctional"}
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Φ
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi_X
            is a fixed datum of the domain (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"}> spacetime
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"}> worldvolume
            ) on which the physical fields are defined. In some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} models
            and for some of the fields this is precisely what one needs, but in other models one may need to be able to also regard the background fields as dynamical fields and to be able to switch between these perspectives, for instance to pass to a setup where what used to be a configuration of some field is now taken to be a fixed background field for the remaining fields. We now discuss how this more general setup is naturally formulated as a generalization of def.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FieldsInAnActionFunctional"}
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Fields
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi BgFields
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Fields
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi BgFields
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Fields} \colon \underset{\mathbf{BgFields}}{\sum} \mathbf{Fields} \to \mathbf{BgFields}
            a morphism in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arrow+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} arrow (∞,1)-topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
