                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo *
                    %mo ≃
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/*} \simeq \mathbf{H}
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} Scalar fields
            are, due to their simplicity, prominent in toy examples used to discuss general properties of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+particle"} Higgs particle
            (or presumably so, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/technicolor"} technicolor
            models it is not actually fundamental but a composite of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            particles, discussed
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Fermions"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electroweak+field"} electroweak
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+group"} SU(2)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} model
            of relevance in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            which crucially features an uncharged scalar particle is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmic inflation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inflaton+field"} inflaton field
            is hypothetical (if it exists at all), it might well be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFT"} effective
            version of non-scalar fields.
          %h4#ParticleTrajectory Particle trajectory
          %p
            The dynamics of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            propagating in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is described by a field on the abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            which is simply a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \to X
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectory"} trajectory
            of the particle. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            that describes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            of such a quantum particle is equivalently a 1-dimensional field theory on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
          %p
            Therefore such wordline
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            fields are given by the special case of def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FieldsInAnActionFunctional"}
            with trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi BgFields
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{BgFields} \simeq *
            and with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Fields
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Fields} \;\colon\; X \to *
            regarded as an object in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo *
